<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hermann Göring</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1893–1946</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Hermann Goering</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hermann Göring was a leading Nazi, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> fighter pilot who became commander of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/luftwaffe">Luftwaffe</a> and Hitler's designated successor. As minister-president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> from 1933 he founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gestapo">Gestapo</a> and used the Prussian police against the regime's opponents. Convicted at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> of war crimes and crimes against humanity, he killed himself in 1946.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hermann-goring.jpg" alt="Hermann Göring" width="320" height="392" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Charles Alexander, Office of the United States Chief of Counsel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hermann_Goering_-_Nuremberg_2_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
